00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.562
So really focusing on this area.

00:04:50.562 --> 00:04:53.020
At Google, the way we think
about the shared responsibility

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:58.210
model is really around two key
items, security of the cloud

00:04:58.210 --> 00:04:59.410
and security in the cloud.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.900
And we'll talk about how
we divvy those things up

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.520
in a moment.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.740
From a core principle
standpoint, security at Google

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.990
is done defense in depth.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:11.350
We have at least two layers
of protections that are

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:14.110
independent from each other
between anything of interest--

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.380
anything you want to protect
and any kind of bad activity

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:18.550
or threat actor--

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:19.550
at scale.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:20.902
So, as you can imagine, Google--

00:05:20.902 --> 00:05:22.360
I think other folks
have said we're

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.760
25% plus of the internet
from a traffic standpoint.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.450
Things done at Google for
security or for other things

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:30.310
have to be done fully
at scale and work

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.320
at scale, and by default. How
do we enable our developers, how

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.850
do we enable the system
so that the controls that

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.640
are necessary and required
are enabled by default so

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.140
that folks can run?

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.030
At the core underlying
this is really

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.840
the use of strong cryptographic
credentials and identity.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.520
And what that means
is that whether it's

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.370
a machine, whether it's
a human, whether it's

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.590
the data, whether it's
the code, whether it's

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:55.145
the underlying
service, they all have

00:05:55.145 --> 00:05:56.770
unique cryptographic
identities that we

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:59.710
can compare against what
should be happening.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.860
And provenance, the ability to
establish, through a hardware

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.710
root of trust, that the
underlying hardware,

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:09.730
the low-level software, the OS
software, the applications that

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:13.600
are running are all fully
attested to at each stage

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.720
and that we're running the
right code at the right time.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:20.200
The other part of this
is that, at Google, we

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.710
think trust isn't gained just
through technology, but also

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.270
through transparency.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:28.120
And really, what that means to
you and to customers in general

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:33.820
is thinking about reducing what
we call the unverifiable trust

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:34.420
surface.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:38.110
What that means is minimizing
the amount you actually

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:40.750
have to trust us as
a cloud provider.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.510
Having you being provided
enough information so

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:45.550
you can make a
decision of your own

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.080
to verify the claims
that we're making.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.680
And that's a core part of
what we're trying to do.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.500
Ultimately, we're interested
in providing customers

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.170
the capabilities to help you
build secure applications

00:06:56.170 --> 00:07:00.160
and fulfill your part of the
shared responsibility model.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.110
When we think about the
controls that are in place,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.890
we really think about
them in three areas.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:07.810
Underlying control,
things that really

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:12.190
help you protect the data;
visibility, the ability

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:15.710
to classify the data and monitor
the actions on that data;

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.680
and then detection
response, the ability

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.450
to actually validate
the controls running

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.210
and detect essentially things
that are non-compliant,

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.930
activities that could be
threats, or from bad actors.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.180
And for each of these
areas, we have technology

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.480
that we provide to enable that.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.100
When we think about the
shared responsibility model,

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.100
it's important to understand
that the boundaries

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.410
of that shared
responsibility vary

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:41.417
based on which types of
services you're using.

00:07:41.417 --> 00:07:43.000
And for many of our
customers, they're

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.010
using a spectrum
of services, which

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:48.560
is why it's not
surprising that, as a CISO

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.300
or as part of the security team,
the details for what service

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.852
and which part can add
to additional complexity.

00:07:55.852 --> 00:07:57.310
And so it's important
to understand

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:58.990
where those divisions are.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:00.430
When you're running on premise--

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:02.553
everything in the blue
represents the customer.

00:08:02.553 --> 00:08:03.970
So when you're
running on premise,

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.273
you own all of your
hardware stack,

00:08:06.273 --> 00:08:08.440
your relationships, the
underlying software running.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.960
So not surprisingly,
the full responsibility

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.890
is really on you
as the customer.

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:15.140
When you're running as an
infrastructure as a service,

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:18.340
which is essentially running
your own virtual machines

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:22.030
on our platform, but
installing your own images,

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.000
setting up your own
network architectures,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.890
the responsibility for us is
to provide you the ability

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:30.730
to log and audit the things
that are running on the system--

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:33.549
the network, the underlying
hardware and infrastructure,

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:35.530
the pieces that you're
building on top of.

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:38.117
So the services we
provide to you are secure,

00:08:38.117 --> 00:08:40.659
and then you're responsible for
putting those things together

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:42.415
in secure architectures.

00:08:42.415 --> 00:08:44.290
When you're running a
platform as a service--

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.080
like BigQuery or App Engine,
one of those things--

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:49.810
now, really, you're providing
as code that you're running.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.090
And we're responsible for
delivering the underlying

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:55.630
services that execute that
code in a safe and secure way.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:57.790
And when you're using
software that we've provided,

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.780
now we're responsible,
as well, for the code.

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:03.550
So, if you're using
Gmail or Drive or Docs,

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:06.070
that code has then become
part of our responsibility.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:07.570
And then the thing
that you focus on

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:10.822
is access to those
applications, access to the data

00:09:10.822 --> 00:09:12.280
that you provide,
and then the data

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.200
that you're loading
into those applications.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.630
That's a quick summary
of the different levels,

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:20.040
and we'll go into some
of this in more detail.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.740
So, as we talked about
from a Google perspective,

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.090
we think about security of
the cloud and in the cloud.

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:29.210
Of the cloud is about
the core infrastructure

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:30.710
that you're then
using, and the core

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:34.190
services to build your
products and applications

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:36.990
for your consumers.

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:40.380
For Google, we're responsible
for security of the cloud,

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.980
and we think about it as, again,
defense, in depth, at scale,

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:47.850
by default. We model the
underlying architecture

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.760
that you're running on
top of in nine layers.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.560
Everything from the
hardware all the way

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:56.190
through the underlying low-level
software, the applications,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:59.070
and ultimately to usage.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.230
And everything that
we do at Google

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:04.380
is rooted in a hardware root
of trust, which is the Titan

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.720
chip that you've heard of
through the last couple

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.100
of Next conferences.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.910
It's a purpose-built
chip that we've built

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:15.330
and it sits on our compute
cards, our processor cards.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:18.990
And establishes, on boot,
a cryptographic identity

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:21.720
for that piece of
hardware and verifies

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.740
that all the underlying
pieces of software

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:26.640
that are meant to boot are
doing so in the right way,

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.670
that the hardware
components are correct

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.230
and configured the right way.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:32.820
And, if any of
those things fail,

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:35.460
that card or that
compute instance

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:39.050
does not actually boot and
be entered into the fleet.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:41.040
And that allows us to
make sure, once something

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.740
goes through that, that
it's in a known good state

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.840
and it can be put into
the rest of the fleet

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:49.140
and start serving customers.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.480
That becomes the
underlying piece

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:54.870
that drives our servers,
our storage, our network,

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.030
and then ultimately
our data centers.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:58.530
And what that allows
us to do, then,

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:01.102
is reduce the threat
for folks injecting

00:11:01.102 --> 00:11:03.060
either malicious hardware
or malicious software

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:03.768
into our systems.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:09.370
In combination with that, as we
talked about in the beginning,

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:12.820
it's important for
us at Google that we

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:15.460
have the ability for
the code that we write

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.130
to define that the
right identity accesses

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:22.870
the right machine, is authorized
by the right code accessing

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:26.110
the right data, and it's in
the right time and context.

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:28.270
And that's done through
cryptographically secure

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:29.240
identities.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.960
So not only users
have identities.

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:34.330
Machines and services
have identities,

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.100
devices have
identities, and then

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:37.600
code and data have identities.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.570
And checking all of those things
when we run a Google service

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:44.190
is a core part of our
underlying platform.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.640
Here's a stack with
a little more detail

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:48.530
of the different
pieces within Google

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:51.080
that come into play
to secure each layer.

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:53.840
You'll see that there's at least
two items at each layer that

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:59.270
are put together to provide
redundancy and really defense

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:00.290
in depth.

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:03.500
Whether it's the purpose-built
underlying infrastructure

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:06.520
or the fact that we,
from a boot standpoint,

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:10.460
cryptographically sign all the
pieces of our boot software,

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:13.440
from an operating system
and hypervisor standpoint,

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.988
we use our own version of
KVM where we stripped out

00:12:16.988 --> 00:12:18.530
a lot of parts of
the virtual machine

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:21.990
monitor, reduced its attack
surface and its risk surface.

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:25.520
We've also then added
sandboxing in so that further

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:27.170
creates a level of isolation.

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:30.230
From an OS standpoint, we
use our own curated operating

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:31.850
system for our host side.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:35.000
We also make that available
as the container-optimized OS

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.220
for you, as a customer, to use.

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:39.410
If you're using our
container-optimized OS,

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.820
you can also enable
automatic updates,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.420
which will patch the
container-optimized OS so you

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:46.880
can leverage and benefit from
the same type of security

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.850
controls we run
on our host side.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:52.820
From a storage and
network standpoint,

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.600
unique to cloud providers,
all services, all data,

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:01.280
is encrypted at rest and
in transit at Google.

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:05.990
The logging of both internal
Googler access, as well as

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:09.170
providing audit logs for
your users and your systems

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:10.955
and what they do
in your systems.

00:13:10.955 --> 00:13:12.890
An identity access
management system

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:15.770
that allows us to do
fine-grain permissions,

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:17.660
and then really
managing those keys

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:20.300
that are core part of
encryption at scale.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:22.790
From a networking side, we
have one of the world's largest

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:23.780
private networks.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:28.460
What that means is that from
when your data or your service

00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:31.820
is touched by a customer
from outside our network,

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:35.540
it basically travels wholly
on Google-owned private

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:39.650
networking, private fiber,
to the server in our systems.

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:42.080
And that gives you not only
performance advantages,

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.960
but gives you a single threat
to choke from a network security

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:45.980
standpoint.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:49.910
There are no additional
actors in the underlying hops.

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:53.180
From an application standpoint,
both from the whole software

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:58.880
lifecycle, from static analysis
to the patching and checking

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:03.600
of the packages that are loaded
in, we have control over that.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.550
We also cryptographically
sign each piece of software

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:09.500
as it goes through
the development stage,

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:13.520
and then check before it's
deployed that the manifest has

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:15.422
all the right policies applied.

00:14:15.422 --> 00:14:16.880
That's something
we've externalized

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:19.610
to customers as a part
of binary authorization.

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:21.740
So, if you want to adopt
that same model that you

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:25.160
can run vulnerability
checks, various types

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.920
of patching checks, static
code analysis, and then

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:31.070
as long as code passes that
created cryptographic signature

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:33.290
and have that checked
before the code is deployed,

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:36.380
you're able to do that through
our binary authorization

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:38.242
product.

00:14:38.242 --> 00:14:39.950
Once the applications
are up and running,

00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:42.110
they're protected by
our own global front

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:46.070
end and the WAF capabilities and
DDoS capabilities around that.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:49.490
And then we provide, as well,
our own security scanning

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:53.450
for L7 web application
vulnerabilities

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.630
that we use inside of Google as
a product called Cloud Security

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:59.120
Scanner for you to
use as a customer.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:00.800
When things are
deployed at scale,

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:04.760
we're very fond of saying that
your first users are typically

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:05.640
abusers.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.990
So whenever we deploy a service,
we see a lot of attack traffic.

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:11.960
Therefore, the built-in
DDoS for our own services,

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.030
the ability to be our own CA.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:17.750
So it's very hard for folks
to forge anything related

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:19.220
to connecting to Google.

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:23.740
And that all services provided
at Google are at full TLS.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:25.780
Finally, from an
operations standpoint,

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:28.030
not just trusting us,
but having third parties

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:29.800
do compliance checking.

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:31.780
The ability to do
live migration, which

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:35.320
enables us to do patching
of live running VMs

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:38.620
without taking your
services down, allows us to,

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.810
on a continuous
basis, do that level

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:43.420
of patching and keeping
things up to the highest

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:44.710
level of security.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:48.130
Then we have full SOC threat
analysis and the ability,

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:51.100
from a user standpoint,
to connect to our services

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:54.520
through beyond corporate
digital trust type network,

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:58.420
as well as for them to do
hardware-based second factor

00:15:58.420 --> 00:15:59.860
for security key.

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:03.490
And that is the structure for
every service created at Google

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:04.288
and used at Google.

00:16:04.288 --> 00:16:05.830
And the tools in
which you would then

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:09.040
build on top of when you
use one of our services.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:11.050
When we think about
enabling customers,

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:12.760
we do this providing
the same type

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:16.870
of services either in the
dark green as products

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:20.300
you can consume, in the light
green as either products

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:24.160
that you can use from Google or
that you can get from partners,

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:28.480
or in the case of the dark
blue, core things we still

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:31.600
do by default for you when
you're running on Google Cloud

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:33.640
Platform.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:36.020
We're going to
talk and highlight

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:39.280
some of the key differentiators
on Google Cloud Platform that

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:42.700
will help you do your
job better and fulfill

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:44.800
your part of the
shared responsibility

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:47.350
from a visibility
and control side.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:49.690
So one of the core
things which we announced

00:16:49.690 --> 00:16:51.940
to general availability
this morning

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:53.860
is the Cloud Security
Command Center.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:55.720
That's that central
pane of glass

00:16:55.720 --> 00:17:00.070
that you can bring in detections
from Google native products,

00:17:00.070 --> 00:17:03.160
third-party products, or
ones you've written yourself

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:05.980
for vulnerabilities and
threats all in one place,

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:08.470
and see those in
context with your assets

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:10.960
and the business context
of those services

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:13.210
and the sensitivity of the data.

00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:15.970
Gives you one place to look for
visibility, one thing for you

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:18.760
to then query, understand your
data, and then the ability

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:22.440
to trigger prevention,
detection, and action.

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:25.770
In addition, what we feel is
a key part of understanding

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:28.200
the attack surface
is understanding

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:31.830
where your data is and what
sensitivity level [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:34.560
What we provide is the same
service we used within Google

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:39.120
for data classification,
our cloud DLP API, which

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:43.020
is designed to work at data at
exabyte and petabyte scales,

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.810
which allows you then
to, number one, for data

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:49.590
sitting in Google Cloud Storage
or data sitting in Bigtable--

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:53.280
BigQuery tables-- to
classify, at scale,

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.930
sensitive data either through
our built-in classifiers,

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:59.340
the ability to use
regular expressions,

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.170
or to express this
in terms of data

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:03.640
sets that we can
train our models on.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.200
Once you've
identified that data,

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:07.920
you then have the choice of
multiple de-identification

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:08.880
options.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:11.400
Simple things like
substitution encryption,

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:15.210
more intricate things like
format preserving encryption,

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.400
or transformations that
allow the data still

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:24.030
to be usable in analytics, but
still parallel serve privacy.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:27.810
In addition, unique to Google is
we provide Access Transparency,

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:30.330
which means that we
provide you, in our audit

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:34.170
logs, a notice every time a
Googler accesses your data

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.090
and for what reason.

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.160
What region that
Googler was from

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.860
and the case number, in the case
of customer-initiated support

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:44.145
events that triggered
that access.

00:18:44.145 --> 00:18:46.590
This is across a wide
range of Google services,

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:50.820
and unlike some of the other
providers, not just restricted

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:54.240
to a small set of the
provided services.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.490
Also, at Next, we're
very excited to announce

00:18:56.490 --> 00:19:00.270
access approvals, which now
allows you to prevent Googlers

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:03.702
from accessing data in real
time for a subset of the data.

00:19:03.702 --> 00:19:05.910
So not only will we notify
you if a Googler is trying

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:09.630
to access data, you can
actually get the ability

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:12.240
to say you will not allow
that and grant that access,

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.330
and the Googler will no longer
have access to that data

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.438
and will not start having
access to that data.

00:19:18.438 --> 00:19:20.730
The results of these are
surfaced in the Cloud Security

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:21.480
Command Center.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:25.250
Also through the
audit logging APIs.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:27.140
One of the key parts
we talked about earlier

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:30.140
was trust through transparency.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:32.133
And so part of that is
there's a lot of things

00:19:32.133 --> 00:19:33.800
that we've told you
about our technology

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:35.130
and how we do things.

00:19:35.130 --> 00:19:37.340
But you don't have
to just trust us.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:41.850
We go through, twice a year,
a set of certifications.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:43.100
You'll see some of these here.

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:46.250
And those certifications
go on an ongoing basis.

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:49.220
Working with your sales teams
and with your account teams,

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:51.890
you can get access to some of
the reports of these things.

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:55.580
So you can see for
yourself, whether it's

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:59.540
overall global standards or
country-specific standards,

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:01.640
how we do against
the requirements

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.940
and certifications you need
to run your businesses.

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:09.190
When we move to control, one
of the key things that's unique

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:12.940
to Google is an emphasis on
providing out-of-the-box,

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:18.550
top-down, logically central, but
globally distributed controls.

00:20:18.550 --> 00:20:20.290
We were the first of
the cloud providers

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:24.130
to provide organization level
viewpoint, a top-down resource

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:27.760
hierarchy that also
is an IAM boundary

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:30.940
and also is a network boundary
so that you have the ability

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:34.600
to start your systems and
your developers in a safe mode

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:37.390
where they have, by
design, restricted access

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:39.250
that you then can
explicitly only

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:41.470
grant through the IAM roles.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:46.075
From an IAM standpoint, we have
over 300 curated roles so out

00:20:46.075 --> 00:20:47.800
of the box, the
separation of duties

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:50.950
are built in by those
products requiring explicitly

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:52.780
grants for folks to use that.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:55.810
We have hierarchy
and inheritance

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:57.020
in our resource hierarchy.

00:20:57.020 --> 00:20:59.590
So if you're given
something at a folder level,

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:03.850
that person then can have
the underlying roles in each

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.200
of the projects below that.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:07.810
But if you're at
a project level,

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:10.840
you can't go up the chain
unless you're explicitly

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:13.130
granted that type of access.

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:14.890
In addition, we
have org policies

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:17.410
that you can define
at the top level that

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:19.850
can be enforced across
all your organization,

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:21.677
regardless of the
underlying pieces.

00:21:21.677 --> 00:21:24.010
And then we're going to talk
about two other things that

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:26.260
affect your
communication pattern.

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:29.050
VPC Service Controls,
which provide you

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:31.570
the ability to define
service perimeters.

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:34.300
So unlike the traditional
L3, L4 networks,

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:36.420
which are based
on routes and IPs,

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:41.530
and restrictions based on IPs,
as you move to microservices,

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:43.810
services then have
unique identities

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:46.750
and you have the ability
to block services access

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:49.680
to particular types of
sensitive data or projects.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:51.760
And those can be applied
to Google services

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:54.920
as well so that you can block,
for example, Google Cloud

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:58.000
Storage rights or reads from
a particular set of resources

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:01.580
only unless those
services either belong

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:04.300
to a particular access level
or have a particular set

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:06.640
of permission groups.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.550
In addition, we also
provide a similar set

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:12.760
of capabilities around your
L3, L4 level networking.

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:14.490
So we have the ability
for you to create

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.940
what's called a shared VPC
and, in that shared VPC,

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:19.560
define all the
firewall rules that

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:22.170
apply to all the tenant VPCs.

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:25.020
So you have, again,
one logical choke point

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.450
where you can provide
the administration

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:31.050
of your overall network,
separate and independent

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.030
from the underlying
administrators

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.490
of the underlying projects.

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:37.980
Both of these
concepts are designed

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:40.530
to provide you
logical bottlenecks

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:44.135
and choke points for
control, but not cost you

00:22:44.135 --> 00:22:46.635
anything on the performance
side because they're implemented

00:22:46.635 --> 00:22:48.780
in globally distributed ways.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:51.250
And those are unique to Google.

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:54.020
VPC Service Controls,
as I talked about,

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:56.960
is really defining
service-level perimeters, which

00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:59.810
allow you to put that
around specific sets

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:03.560
of sensitive data, allow
you to define access levels.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.390
And those access
levels allow you

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:07.430
to put policy
requirements, for example,

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:11.630
of what kind of geographies that
the data can be accessed from,

00:23:11.630 --> 00:23:15.140
what kind of restricted
IPs, what potential device

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:17.780
characteristics are needed
to access that data.

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:20.300
Those access context
levels apply not only

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:22.550
to infrastructure
controls like this,

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:27.500
but also back into your G
Suite access to Docs and Gmail.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:31.130
So in one construct,
access levels,

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:33.080
you can define a set
of controls that apply

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:34.970
across both G Suite and GCP.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:41.410
Similarly, from a key
management standpoint,

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:43.480
as I talked about,
by default, your data

00:23:43.480 --> 00:23:46.000
is encrypted at
rest and in transit.

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:48.160
But we know for some
of our customers,

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:52.090
they have additional either
regular requirements or views

00:23:52.090 --> 00:23:54.410
of their threat
model or risk that

00:23:54.410 --> 00:23:56.800
require greater
control of their keys,

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:58.510
so we provide a full spectrum.

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:00.580
From the left,
default encryption,

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:03.260
you don't have to do anything.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.020
Your data is encrypted at rest.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:07.000
Cloud key management
system, which

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:09.310
allows you full control
of creation of keys,

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:12.490
destruction of keys,
rotation, times, and periods.

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:13.510
Full logging.

00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:16.390
IAM roles on those keys
so you can demonstrate

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:18.220
to your regulators
and auditors that you

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:20.170
have full control of the keys.

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:23.470
Cloud HSM, which is a
hardware-backed solution

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:26.950
so that you can have a root
of trust for those KMS keys

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:28.480
be in hardware.

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:31.660
And it's a Phipps Level 3
hardware, globally distributed

00:24:31.660 --> 00:24:34.060
Cloud HCM system.

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:37.570
You can also, if you need
to have the root keys

00:24:37.570 --> 00:24:39.940
be sourced within
your own org, you

00:24:39.940 --> 00:24:44.740
can use cloud's essentially
customer-supplied encryption

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:48.970
keys, which allows you to push
the encryption keys to us when

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:50.980
you need something decrypted.

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:52.680
We do not store that key.

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:55.230
It stays only in memory
for the live operation

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:56.980
and, therefore, you
have not only the root

00:24:56.980 --> 00:24:59.590
of trust in your
own hardware system,

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:02.500
we have no access to
those underlying keys.

00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:06.370
You can also have, essentially,
the ability to stack

00:25:06.370 --> 00:25:10.040
your own HSMs in our Colos if
you want that further level,

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:12.640
additional control on
essentially customer-supplied

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:13.880
encryption keys.

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:16.613
So depending on the risk
parameters of your business,

00:25:16.613 --> 00:25:18.280
you have a full
spectrum of capabilities

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:22.360
available to support
data encryption.

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:24.160
And before I turn
it over to Dan,

00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:27.310
I'm going to talk through now
how the various controls get

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:30.660
put together when you think
about a secure architecture.

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:34.040
So first, most customers
start with a clean slate.

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:37.730
They have an existing on premise
or other cloud installation.

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:41.210
They have identities set up,
typically not a Google identity

00:25:41.210 --> 00:25:43.250
out of the box, from an
enterprise standpoint,

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:46.080
local compute,
gateways, and then data.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:50.090
So the first thing to do
is to essentially connect

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:53.510
to cloud identity, which lets us
federate your existing identity

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:54.290
service.

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:55.850
And now brings those
identities to be

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:58.697
used in Google Cloud Platform.

00:25:58.697 --> 00:26:01.030
Then, through the groups and
the underlying individuals,

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:03.480
assign out the IAM
roles and policies

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:06.050
which you want so that folks
have the correct separation

00:26:06.050 --> 00:26:07.440
of duties.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:10.040
The ability to establish
upfront org-level policies

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:12.590
that apply, regardless
of the underlying pieces,

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:14.360
across your whole organization.

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:18.350
For example, that VMs cannot
have publicly-facing internet

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:20.630
addresses.

00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:22.430
Then the ability
to define, again,

00:26:22.430 --> 00:26:26.360
from a L3, L4 level, and
overall control of your firewall

00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:30.350
rules and networking
through shared VPCs.

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:32.960
Breaking out from an HA
standpoint and a disaster

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:35.600
recovery standpoint, the
various regional pieces

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:39.170
that you want to allocate within
each of the regions, the zones,

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:41.750
the resource allocation, and
then the underlying subnet

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:42.560
connections.

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:45.380
By default, and
unique to Google,

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:47.135
our networks are global.

00:26:47.135 --> 00:26:49.010
So you're able to make
those connections very

00:26:49.010 --> 00:26:49.800
straightforward.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:52.490
You don't have to pair
between the different regions.

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:55.400
Our VPCs are global by
nature, and you're able to,

00:26:55.400 --> 00:26:59.690
therefore, set up HA and DR
in a very straightforward way.

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:01.190
Next thing you want
to do ultimately

00:27:01.190 --> 00:27:02.960
is you have data
sitting near on prem,

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:05.720
and you want to bring
it securely into GCP

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:09.320
or take the data that we compute
and bring it back to your

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:10.140
on prem.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:13.400
So combining Cloud
Router, firewall rules,

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:17.180
dedicated secure interconnect,
either through our own peering

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:18.860
or through partner
peering, allows

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:21.290
you to bring the data in and
out of your area securely.

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:25.230
Also, we have VPNs to allow
you to do that as well.

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:27.710
Next piece, then, is from
an application standpoint.

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:29.870
Protect your application
once you serve it

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:32.360
from the typical
attacks like DDoS.

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:34.850
So you can either
use our default

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:36.920
global load balancer,
which as long as you

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:39.370
put that in front of one
of your GCE services,

00:27:39.370 --> 00:27:42.680
will take advantage of
our global front end.

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.870
You can use one of our
managed services, which

00:27:44.870 --> 00:27:47.970
has DDoS built in, or you
can use the Cloud Armor

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:49.970
product, which gives you
additional availability

00:27:49.970 --> 00:27:52.760
and the ability to write
your own rules to fine-grain

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:55.460
manage your DDoS and WAF.

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:58.760
Next piece, then, is you
want to turn on logging

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:01.250
so that you have the monitoring
and evidence of things

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.710
that might happen to
allow us to do detections.

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:06.870
And audit logs are
turned on by default.

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:10.070
You can then also look at
VPC Flow Logs, firewall logs,

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.497
and then general logging.

00:28:11.497 --> 00:28:13.580
Then you want to turn on
the monitoring, learning,

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:16.310
and detection pieces, which
include the Cloud Security

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:20.360
Command Center, security
health analytics, the ability

00:28:20.360 --> 00:28:22.280
to look at stackdriver
monitoring,

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:25.400
running security scanner
on your, essentially, web

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:26.425
applications.

00:28:26.425 --> 00:28:28.550
And then if you want to
dump the data into BigQuery

00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:32.100
for additional processing.

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:33.893
You then create a
service project,

00:28:33.893 --> 00:28:35.310
which allows you
to kind of create

00:28:35.310 --> 00:28:39.090
that boundary of control for
the services you want to run.

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:42.570
And then create kind of a secure
perimeter using VP service

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:46.080
controls around that so that you
can restrict which services can

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:48.240
access that underlying data.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:54.030
And then, ultimately, bring
those data back into GCP

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:59.070
through either private access,
Cloud DNS, and cloud natting.

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:03.330
So you can either place
your subnets into GCP

00:29:03.330 --> 00:29:06.030
or push our subnet pieces out.

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:08.790
So, in summary,
those are the steps

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:12.480
that you take to pull things
in and are the building

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:15.593
blocks of you building
a secure service on GCP,

00:29:15.593 --> 00:29:17.010
and some of the
pieces we give you

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:19.740
to fulfill your part of the
shared responsibility model.

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:21.365
So with that, I'm
going to turn it over

00:29:21.365 --> 00:29:24.496
to Dan to talk to you about
Capital One's journey.

00:29:24.496 --> 00:29:26.070
DANIEL HYMEL: Great.

00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:26.570
Thank you.

00:29:26.570 --> 00:29:28.263
Are we using this mic?

00:29:31.221 --> 00:29:33.220
ANDY CHANG: No, we can
use your mic for this.

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:34.290
DANIEL HYMEL: OK, great.

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:40.790
Andy, thank you for
opening the stage

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:42.890
and allowing me to
present and open

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:48.110
the windows on what's actually
going on inside of Capital One.

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:51.050
So I promised you
a cautionary tale.

00:29:51.050 --> 00:29:55.430
And this little
creature's name is Altria.

00:29:55.430 --> 00:29:59.630
Altria is a rescue dog that my
12-year-old daughter picked up

00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:03.770
from the Richmond Animal
League in Richmond, Virginia.

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:06.950
What does this dog
have anything to do

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:10.820
with the topic of shared
responsibilities, compliance,

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:11.960
and controls?

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:15.230
On the cover, probably
absolutely nothing.

00:30:15.230 --> 00:30:17.390
But take a closer look.

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:19.880
Take a look at what's
around her neck.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:21.950
So here's the tale.

00:30:21.950 --> 00:30:25.970
It happened shortly
after Google '18.

00:30:25.970 --> 00:30:30.610
About a week after Google
'18, this little dog, Altria--

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:31.460
love her to death.

00:30:31.460 --> 00:30:34.100
She has no malicious
intent whatsoever.

00:30:34.100 --> 00:30:35.160
But she's a runner.

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:36.860
She was trained
as a hunting dog.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:39.200
She's an American hound.

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:43.910
So on one particular night, this
was probably after the fifth

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:46.730
escape from the homestead--
we live on a parcel out

00:30:46.730 --> 00:30:47.960
in the country--

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:49.610
we kept talking
about, let's put it

00:30:49.610 --> 00:30:52.610
in a security fence,
an invisible fence.

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:56.340
And we kept holding off
because the price was too high.

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:59.600
So on one particular event,
she left around 8:00 PM.

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:01.760
She has free run of
the neighborhood.

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:04.220
We live in this river
canyon on the James River,

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:07.370
where about a mile on either
side, there are no fences.

00:31:07.370 --> 00:31:09.140
And you can run from
the Chesapeake Bay

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:11.180
all the way to the mountains.

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:14.750
On this particular day, it
was about 11:00 PM at night.

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:15.650
We were exhausted.

00:31:15.650 --> 00:31:16.970
We couldn't find her.

00:31:16.970 --> 00:31:18.570
We came home.

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:21.320
And as I was planning
to go around the house

00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:26.660
to check things out, I felt
this immense pain in my leg

00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:29.460
and I jumped and wondered
what the heck happened.

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:32.490
So I looked down and on
the corner of the eye,

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:35.330
there was a copperhead snake.

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:39.990
Unfortunately, that snake took
advantage of the opportunity.

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:43.970
And as you know, later that day,
I was in the paramedics box.

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:45.620
I was at the hospital.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:48.960
I was being treated for
a copperhead snake bite.

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:51.360
So as you know,
in everyday life,

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:54.790
there are risk
scenarios everywhere.

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:56.400
This is a very good example.

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:59.550
Had we actually put
in the security fence,

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:02.550
we would've avoided
Altria from escaping

00:32:02.550 --> 00:32:05.520
from our private domain
out into the public.

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:07.830
So as a result, we
implemented this control.

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:09.270
It's a shock caller.

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:11.880
So as you approach
the fence, she

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:14.460
gets a really
enlightening experience.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:18.190
If she's able to go beyond that
barrier into the public domain,

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:20.580
there are some other controls
that we've implemented.

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:24.870
So what you might not
know about the rescue

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:26.940
dogs is that when
you receive them,

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:29.460
you receive them with a chip.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:32.610
So if they are
captured in public,

00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:34.620
there is a way to
identify those.

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:38.180
We also added a collar or a tag.

00:32:38.180 --> 00:32:40.890
The tag used to have only
our home phone number,

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.020
but she runs so much
that we actually

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:45.720
had to put a cell phone number
because we would get phone

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:48.900
calls at home when we're
actually out in the field

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:50.850
looking for her.

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:55.800
So, as you see now, it has a
lot to do with cloud controls--

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:58.570
I'm sorry-- with controls,
with shared responsibilities.

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:01.110
It's the whole community.

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:03.400
So let me take you into
the Capital One journey.

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:05.640
I'm going to open the
window for you for a moment.

00:33:05.640 --> 00:33:06.960
You won't see this often.

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:08.910
And I'm going to tell
you and share with you

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:11.820
a few nuggets on how
we made our journey

00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:13.820
into the cloud successful.

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:17.220
So Capital One, today, has
a significant need for cloud

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:20.280
because it enables us to
operate with the speed

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:23.970
and agility required to
succeed in the digital age.

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:27.030
Our agile sprint teams
work in two-week cycles,

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:29.010
and according to
them, infrastructure

00:33:29.010 --> 00:33:31.650
must not be an impediment.

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:34.500
As a result of moving to the
cloud, there are some stats,

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:36.540
and these are real numbers.

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:38.310
We've moved from a
development environment

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:41.880
build time of three
months down to 30 minutes.

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:44.520
New product features
which used to take a month

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:47.240
now take two days.

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:50.820
With unparalleled visibility
into our environments,

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:53.850
we have an overall
improved security posture.

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:56.900
I'm going to talk back on
security in just a moment.

00:33:56.900 --> 00:33:58.760
Our current cloud
status is this.

00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:02.940
We have over 8,000 production
applications and services.

00:34:02.940 --> 00:34:06.110
6,000 additional
services and applications

00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:07.610
are in non-production.

00:34:07.610 --> 00:34:09.920
And nearly 100%
of our production

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:14.699
in development and test
servers are in the cloud.

00:34:14.699 --> 00:34:16.830
Additionally,
moving to the cloud

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:19.500
has increased our ability
to secure and manage

00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:24.770
massive volumes of
high-quality data,

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:28.370
operating at a higher speed
with greater resiliency, faster

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:34.120
recovery, and at an
extraordinarily lower cost.

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:36.340
With Cloud, our
technology organization

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:39.880
is free to do what we do best,
build digital breakthrough

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:42.100
experiences for our customer.

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:45.270
If you are a customer
of Capital One,

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:47.820
feel comfortable in knowing
that we have your back

00:34:47.820 --> 00:34:50.850
and we are doing our very
best using state of the art

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:56.090
technologies and best practices
to protect your sensitive data.

00:34:56.090 --> 00:35:00.020
To make this happen, to make
this security posture happen,

00:35:00.020 --> 00:35:03.950
we've implemented a methodology
around controls and compliance.

00:35:03.950 --> 00:35:07.040
A lot of organizations might
call this cloud governance

00:35:07.040 --> 00:35:09.200
or a governance function.

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:10.760
So essentially,
that's what it is.

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:13.430
It's an enterprise
function at Capital One

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:16.550
that balances the
need of compliance

00:35:16.550 --> 00:35:20.000
of cloud controls,
objectives, and the needs

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:22.700
of various stakeholders,
from our board of directors

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:26.060
to executive management, to
the enterprise communities,

00:35:26.060 --> 00:35:29.840
like operations, engineering,
info security, and cyber--

00:35:29.840 --> 00:35:33.080
all of the communities
that come together to help

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:36.620
enable our security
capabilities in the cloud.

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:39.150
And most importantly,
our divisional customers.

00:35:39.150 --> 00:35:41.330
These are our
lines of business--

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:45.560
our banking community, our
auto finance community,

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:46.970
our card community.

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:50.360
These are the application
builders and consumers

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:52.440
of cloud services.

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:54.680
Another way to look at
cloud control and compliance

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:58.850
is it's the totality set
of policies and procedures

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:02.150
that help an enterprise
move towards its goals

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:04.970
while minimizing
risk and conflicts.

00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:10.070
So for us, our journey here,
our goal is all in on the cloud,

00:36:10.070 --> 00:36:13.010
and that's just
about where we are.

00:36:13.010 --> 00:36:16.480
So let's go back to, then,
what is this cloud compliance

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:17.440
function?

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:23.230
What I'm going to share with
you is a best practice example

00:36:23.230 --> 00:36:26.380
that is aligned very closely
with the Cloud Security

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:28.510
Alliance methodology.

00:36:28.510 --> 00:36:33.880
So think of this as
a box of cake mix,

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:35.830
where you flip that
box on the backside

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:38.350
and it gives you
all the ingredients.

00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:40.720
These are all the ingredients
to make it happen.

00:36:40.720 --> 00:36:42.830
These are the best practices.

00:36:42.830 --> 00:36:45.610
You have to include all
of these, in my opinion,

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:47.410
in your program for
cloud compliance

00:36:47.410 --> 00:36:50.740
to have the maximum benefit
to ensure that you're

00:36:50.740 --> 00:36:52.720
doing the appropriate
level of due diligence

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:55.420
for your environment.

00:36:55.420 --> 00:36:57.460
I'll go by these one
by one, and then I'll

00:36:57.460 --> 00:37:00.410
give you some more detail
on what they actually mean.

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:02.890
So first off, you must
document and maintain

00:37:02.890 --> 00:37:05.450
a catalog of control objectives.

00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:10.720
These objectives are partially
derived from Nest 800-53,

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:12.750
or FedRAMP Moderate.

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:15.070
They are also a
compilation or a derivative

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:19.540
of your internal security
policies and procedures.

00:37:19.540 --> 00:37:22.180
Secondly, for every
single service

00:37:22.180 --> 00:37:25.180
that our cloud
providers offer, we

00:37:25.180 --> 00:37:28.150
perform a security
control assessment

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:31.360
using a selected set
of security control

00:37:31.360 --> 00:37:33.250
objectives in that catalog.

00:37:33.250 --> 00:37:36.340
And, for now, our current
status for our catalog

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:39.520
of about 300 control
objectives, we use about 50

00:37:39.520 --> 00:37:43.600
of those that are directly
applicable to cloud

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:46.450
as it relates to our
shared responsibilities.

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:50.050
And, of those 50, 15 of
the control objectives

00:37:50.050 --> 00:37:53.440
are mandatory
requirements, meaning that,

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:54.640
if the service provider--

00:37:54.640 --> 00:37:56.980
Google-- provides
a service to us

00:37:56.980 --> 00:38:00.790
and it doesn't meet any of
the 15, that's a showstopper.

00:38:00.790 --> 00:38:04.130
So we'll just tell Google,
we're not ready yet.

00:38:04.130 --> 00:38:06.340
We need some more features
before it's safe for us

00:38:06.340 --> 00:38:08.760
to consume that service.

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:13.080
Next, once you've done
the security assessments,

00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:16.710
you move into the phase of
performing the risk analysis

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:18.840
on the gaps that you've
identified relative

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.410
to your control objectives.

00:38:21.410 --> 00:38:23.710
So depending on which
industry that you're in,

00:38:23.710 --> 00:38:25.800
you may have a different
set of objectives

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:29.760
than what Capital One has as
a financial service provider.

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:35.190
Today, we're using a composition
of qualitative and quantitative

00:38:35.190 --> 00:38:36.480
methodologies.

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:40.870
We're looking very closely at
probabilistic risk analysis,

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:44.100
which means that there is
a probability in a risk

00:38:44.100 --> 00:38:47.550
scenario of an event happening
over an annualized time

00:38:47.550 --> 00:38:53.630
period causing a loss of
money in a particular range.

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:56.050
So now that you've
identified your gaps,

00:38:56.050 --> 00:38:58.210
now comes the tough part.

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:02.170
This is a continuous process
on risk management, control

00:39:02.170 --> 00:39:05.380
implementation, control
compliance, control exception

00:39:05.380 --> 00:39:06.370
processing.

00:39:06.370 --> 00:39:08.980
You have to implement
your controls.

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:11.620
You have to monitor your
controls for compliance.

00:39:11.620 --> 00:39:14.800
And you have to allow
application owners

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:18.820
to make exceptions against using
the services that might not

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:20.950
have all the controls
or for which you're not

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:22.930
able to meet all of those.

00:39:22.930 --> 00:39:24.790
Not a requirement,
but the next one

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:26.770
is the divisional
governance advisor.

00:39:26.770 --> 00:39:28.480
And what we've done
is we've assigned

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:32.110
a technical subject
matter expert like myself

00:39:32.110 --> 00:39:35.695
to support a particular line
of business that translates

00:39:35.695 --> 00:39:37.570
everything that we've
talked about into terms

00:39:37.570 --> 00:39:39.130
that they consume.

00:39:39.130 --> 00:39:41.890
And most importantly,
as well, training.

00:39:41.890 --> 00:39:45.460
The associates that
perform in this function

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:48.040
need to have background
as a subject matter

00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:52.060
expert in the cloud
provided environment.

00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:54.770
Back to shared responsibilities
component of this.

00:39:54.770 --> 00:39:58.220
Andy talked ad nauseam about
this-- this is really great--

00:39:58.220 --> 00:40:00.050
so I won't go into
any detail here.

00:40:00.050 --> 00:40:03.220
But I wanted to share with you
that we understand this well,

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:05.440
and I'll just move on
to the next one, which

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:07.210
is a payout slide.

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:10.240
This slide basically
says, for Capital One,

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:12.280
we look at shared
responsibilities

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:17.080
from both a platform perspective
and an application perspective.

00:40:17.080 --> 00:40:20.320
Let's take the application
perspective first.

00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:22.210
I'm sorry, the
platform perspective.

00:40:22.210 --> 00:40:24.400
So my team working
in cloud governance,

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:26.800
we do everything that
we talked about before.

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:28.180
We assess the services.

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:31.840
We ensure that they meet
our security requirements.

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:34.660
We work with cloud
engineering teams

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:36.790
and we provision the
services and make

00:40:36.790 --> 00:40:40.720
those available to our
consumers that leverage

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:43.550
those services by
way of applications.

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:45.460
So we're accountable
for then assuring

00:40:45.460 --> 00:40:48.580
that we implement platform
identity and access

00:40:48.580 --> 00:40:50.650
management, that we
deploy the operating

00:40:50.650 --> 00:40:55.090
systems appropriately, meaning
that we provision system

00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:55.750
images.

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:58.960
Rather than using the
cloud provider images,

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:01.900
we provision our own so
that we appropriately

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:04.390
harden them to meet
our internal standards.

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:06.910
We're accountable for what
we put into the cloud,

00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:09.430
so we take full
responsibility for that.

00:41:09.430 --> 00:41:12.490
One particular example of
a control related to this

00:41:12.490 --> 00:41:15.700
is a notion of rehydration.

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:20.050
So, as a virtual machine ages,
it takes on vulnerabilities

00:41:20.050 --> 00:41:22.600
because the vulnerability
aging process,

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:26.140
and so we have a requirement
to rehydrate every 60 days.

00:41:26.140 --> 00:41:29.050
If you go beyond that,
bells and whistles go off.

00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:32.660
If you go past 90,
bigger whistles go off.

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:37.160
If you go to 120, the
canons go off and so forth.

00:41:37.160 --> 00:41:38.770
So I think you get that.

00:41:38.770 --> 00:41:40.990
So our cloud
governance function,

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:43.810
from a centralized
perspective, is

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:48.700
we make controls and scripts
available that either we

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:51.460
can run on behalf of the
development community,

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:54.490
or that we can give it to them
and they can actually run it.

00:41:54.490 --> 00:41:57.370
For example, there
might be a requirement

00:41:57.370 --> 00:42:00.640
to apply labels or tags
to all of your resources.

00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:03.100
If the development community
forgets to do that,

00:42:03.100 --> 00:42:04.780
they can run a
script to make sure

00:42:04.780 --> 00:42:06.760
that they're compliant to that.

00:42:06.760 --> 00:42:10.700
Let's move on then
to the application.

00:42:10.700 --> 00:42:12.460
If you build it, you own it.

00:42:12.460 --> 00:42:14.560
That's the mantra
at Capital One.

00:42:14.560 --> 00:42:17.500
So if we offer a service
to you, you're golden.

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:18.590
You can use that.

00:42:18.590 --> 00:42:19.600
It's been whitelisted.

00:42:19.600 --> 00:42:24.220
The controls are in place, but
you have some accountability.

00:42:24.220 --> 00:42:27.250
I'll talk a bit more
about that accountability.

00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:29.180
So why are cloud
controls important?

00:42:29.180 --> 00:42:31.980
I wanted to make sure that you
had full visibility to that.

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:34.150
For Capital One,
it's our mission

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:36.800
and our fiduciary
responsibility.

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:41.020
If you take a standard document
classification or information

00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:45.040
handling matrix,
you will find that

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:47.620
your proprietary or
confidential data is probably

00:42:47.620 --> 00:42:50.740
your most important asset and
requires a level of protection

00:42:50.740 --> 00:42:54.190
beyond those that are
company or public.

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:56.950
So what we do and
how we've bifurcated

00:42:56.950 --> 00:43:01.660
this is we look at the controls
in the cloud control catalog

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:03.940
in three particular areas.

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:06.620
There's technical,
operational, and management.

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:10.090
And I'll show you, on the next
slide, how we codify those.

00:43:10.090 --> 00:43:15.250
But most importantly, the
controls have to always be on.

00:43:15.250 --> 00:43:17.710
The windows have to
always be locked.

00:43:17.710 --> 00:43:19.900
So that means
security protocols,

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:24.070
public accessibility, data
encryption, identity access,

00:43:24.070 --> 00:43:25.120
and PCI.

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:27.380
There is no opportunity.

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:32.130
There is no tolerance
for control being off.

00:43:32.130 --> 00:43:35.310
So where do cloud
controls originate?

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:37.230
I mentioned to you a
bit earlier that they

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:41.130
might originate from
cybersecurity frameworks, Nest,

00:43:41.130 --> 00:43:42.610
FedRAMP, and so forth.

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:48.750
Here's a particular small matrix
of the 14 control categories

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:51.840
in the Nest
framework, where I've

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:55.240
bifurcated those by technical,
operational, and management.

00:43:55.240 --> 00:43:58.210
So we take our Cloud
Control catalogs.

00:43:58.210 --> 00:44:00.970
We look at a service
that Google has provided.

00:44:00.970 --> 00:44:04.230
We perform that
security assessment.

00:44:04.230 --> 00:44:06.490
The diagram below, or the
colorful green, red, blue

00:44:06.490 --> 00:44:10.420
chart, is the actual
detail of the questions

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:11.830
that we might ask.

00:44:11.830 --> 00:44:13.780
And, incidentally, I
have to say that Google

00:44:13.780 --> 00:44:16.090
has been an extraordinary
partner in this.

00:44:16.090 --> 00:44:17.770
Google has our scripts.

00:44:17.770 --> 00:44:19.510
They know what we require.

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:24.220
They know when a service
is ready to meet the higher

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:26.500
bar for financial
services industry,

00:44:26.500 --> 00:44:28.570
and so they are embracing that.

00:44:28.570 --> 00:44:32.080
They're working very diligently
to say, hey, Capital One,

00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:34.073
we've got this new service.

00:44:34.073 --> 00:44:35.740
And they know what
our requirements are.

00:44:35.740 --> 00:44:40.400
So they know when to
actually make the ask.

00:44:40.400 --> 00:44:43.060
And finally, we complete
the service assessment.

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:44.320
We deliver the package.

00:44:44.320 --> 00:44:47.260
Now, it's important to
note here, under Item C,

00:44:47.260 --> 00:44:49.570
that it's a
cross-functional team.

00:44:49.570 --> 00:44:52.300
We include governance, we
include engineering resources,

00:44:52.300 --> 00:44:59.080
cyber resources, architecture,
IAM architecture and, also,

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:01.300
our information assurance
third-party management

00:45:01.300 --> 00:45:06.580
function, where
we fully validate.

00:45:06.580 --> 00:45:09.220
We have an auditable
trail that our regulators

00:45:09.220 --> 00:45:11.510
and our internal auditors
can go back and say,

00:45:11.510 --> 00:45:14.170
did you ask that question?

00:45:14.170 --> 00:45:17.240
Google tells a lot of good
things about their services,

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:23.160
but we firmly believe
in trust, but verify.

00:45:23.160 --> 00:45:25.170
So this is the final slide.

00:45:25.170 --> 00:45:27.270
I've got about 15
seconds or so, and then

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:30.900
we'll save time for Q&amp;A.
But here's the payout,

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:34.080
and this is what
resonates with shared

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:37.710
responsibilities from an
application owner perspective.

00:45:37.710 --> 00:45:39.510
Here's what it means.

00:45:39.510 --> 00:45:41.410
If you have an application--

00:45:41.410 --> 00:45:45.300
let's take a basic, multi-tier
architecture application--

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:48.060
where you're going to
need a load balancer,

00:45:48.060 --> 00:45:51.390
a web server, application
server, database and file

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:55.800
storage, that application
or the type of application

00:45:55.800 --> 00:46:00.540
may require that you adhere
to an enterprise architecture

00:46:00.540 --> 00:46:01.110
standard.

00:46:01.110 --> 00:46:03.990
There may be a defined
architecture already.

00:46:03.990 --> 00:46:06.723
The architecture might
even say, in order

00:46:06.723 --> 00:46:08.640
to meet the requirements
for the architecture,

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:10.680
there are certain
cloud services that you

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:12.630
are mandated to follow.

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:15.150
So the application owner
defines the application.

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:16.860
They choose the architecture.

00:46:16.860 --> 00:46:20.550
They select the cloud
services that are approved.

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:22.290
This is by no
means the only list

00:46:22.290 --> 00:46:23.820
of what's approved
at Capital One,

00:46:23.820 --> 00:46:27.090
so we could put dot dot dot.

00:46:27.090 --> 00:46:29.430
After you select the
approved services,

00:46:29.430 --> 00:46:31.710
then you become more
aware of an application

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:35.760
owner of what your actual
requirements for controls are.

00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:39.970
So if you pick a Compute
Engine, if you pick Storage,

00:46:39.970 --> 00:46:42.840
you will have some requirements
for identity access, data

00:46:42.840 --> 00:46:44.710
encryption, and so forth.

00:46:44.710 --> 00:46:47.010
So this is really
the whole story.

00:46:47.010 --> 00:46:48.570
There's so much detail here.

00:46:48.570 --> 00:46:51.160
Feel free to reach
out to me on LinkedIn

00:46:51.160 --> 00:46:53.160
if you have some
follow-up questions.

00:46:53.160 --> 00:46:58.410
And we'll actually go back to
Andy to close it out for us.

00:46:58.410 --> 00:46:59.930
ANDY CHANG: Thank you, Dan.

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:04.140
So in summary, though
one last slide.

00:47:04.140 --> 00:47:05.445
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:09.480
So security of the
cloud is something

00:47:09.480 --> 00:47:11.580
the cloud provider secures.

00:47:11.580 --> 00:47:13.200
Security in the
cloud is something

00:47:13.200 --> 00:47:15.930
that the cloud customer
secures for their data

00:47:15.930 --> 00:47:16.720
and their content.

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:20.130
At Google Cloud, we look to
empower you as a customer

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:22.950
with the visibility to
control and secure the things

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:24.690
that you're responsible for.

00:47:24.690 --> 00:47:26.460
And as Dan talked
about, customers

00:47:26.460 --> 00:47:29.717
can be successful in cloud
using a very thoughtful process,

00:47:29.717 --> 00:47:31.800
structure, and clean
separations of responsibility

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:33.030
within their own org.

00:47:33.030 --> 00:47:36.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

